  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n- Dry and warmer through Tuesday, then rain.\n\nA large area of high pressure was noted over western Quebec this\nafternoon. Skies were clear to the north and west of the high.\nHowever, IR loop revealed cloudy skies to the south of the ridge\naxis where low pressure was located near the Ohio Valley. The \nnorthern edge of the cloud shield has encroached upon the southern\nrow of counties. While much of the cumulus will dissipate with \nsunset, we'll likely see some mid and high clouds overhead tonight\nand tomorrow...most numerous from I-96 southward.\n\nThe medium range models are in good agreement through the weekend\nand confidence is high we'll have dry weather with a slight\nwarming trend through Monday. In fact, all of the ECMWF ensemble\nmembers show no precipitation until next Tuesday. Thus,\ncomfortable early fall weather is upon us with highs in the upper\n60s to lower 70s and dewpoints in the 40s and 50s.\n\nThe blocking upper ridge will begin to break down early next week\nas a northern stream short wave phases with a southern stream\nwave. This combination will carve out a deeper upper trough over\nthe Rockies which will push a cold front toward the Great Lakes\nearly next week. We'll see south flow pull some moisture northward\nresulting in rain showers when the cold front moves across Lake MI\nTuesday night and Wednesday. This far out, the models aren't\nshowing much instability so we'll go with mostly rain at this\npoint Tuesday night and Wednesday.",
